{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 actinn-jax is tied-#1 on the richer within-dataset liver data and in the top cluster on the hard cross-study split; it trails the deep methods by 1\u8211-2 points on the high- cardinality and small-n sets. scmap-cluster is consistently the weakest (0.24 macro-F1 on liver_cross).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b \u8224 ? lung_cross exact accuracy (~0.35 for every method) is a label-vocabulary artifact, not a transfer failure.} HCLA (reference) and Krasnow (query) were annotated independently and share only {\b 20 of 46 cell-type names}; 26 of Krasnow\u8217's types are absent from HCLA\u8217's vocabulary, so no classifier can emit the exact string. The mismatch is granularity: HCLA\u8217's finer taxonomy has {\f1 alveolar / elicited / lung macrophage} but {\b no generic {\f1 macrophage}} (CL:0000235), while Krasnow labels many cells generic {\f1 macrophage} \u8212- so an HCLA-trained model predicts {\f1 lung macrophage} (CL:1001603) for them, exact-wrong but ontology-correct ({\i lung macrophage is-a macrophage}); likewise Krasnow\u8217's generic {\f1 endothelial cell} (CL:0000115) has no HCLA counterpart. {\b Ontology-aware concordance \u8212- which credits a same-lineage call \u8212- is 0.75\u8211-0.77 for every method}, so cross-dataset transfer actually works; exact-match just conflates classifier error with vocabulary/granularity mismatch. This is precisely why we report ontology concordance, and it is a general benchmarking pitfall: {\b cross-dataset exact accuracy between independently-annotated atlases is not a meaningful accuracy signal.} The other five datasets use a single shared label vocabulary for reference and query (a split of one atlas, or HLiCA\u8217's harmonized annotations), so their exact scores are unaffected.\par}
