\intbl\row}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 (*original ACTINN and scANVI do most work in a single train+predict pass, attributed to predict.) The four fast methods \u8212- SVM, kNN, actinn-jax, CellTypist \u8212- predict in well under a second; the deep/correlation/foundation tiers are 25\u8211-200\u215 ? slower. actinn-jax predicts in {\b 0.37 s vs.\u160 ?scANVI\u8217's 73 s at statistically tied accuracy} (~195\u215 ?), and is far lighter than the memory-heavy tiers (original ACTINN 7.7 GB, scmap 8.5 GB). Of the methods that match its accuracy, none matches its speed; of the methods that match its speed (SVM, kNN), none matches its accuracy \u8212- i.e.\u160 ?actinn-jax is Pareto-non-dominated.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The accuracy \u215 ? speed frontier\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw895\pich616\picwgoal9205\pichgoal6336 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Accuracy versus total wall time (liver_intra); actinn-jax sits on the fast frontier.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Plotting accuracy against total wall time makes the frontier explicit: actinn-jax, SVM, kNN, and CellTypist form the efficient (fast) frontier, and among those actinn-jax has the highest accuracy on the richer datasets. The deep methods buy ~0.4 accuracy points on average (up to ~2 points on the 86-type blood+gut set, zero-to-negative on the liver and cross-dataset sets) at 50\u8211-200\u215 ? the inference cost \u8212- the trade the frontier is meant to expose.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 Scaling\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1665\pich615\picwgoal17125\pichgoal6325 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Fit and predict time versus reference size and cardinality; predict time stays flat and sub-second.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Training time grows with reference size and with #cell types for all trained methods \u8212- actinn-jax\u8217's fit goes 13 s \u8594 ? 141 s \u8594 ? 254 s as the reference grows 965 \u8594 ? 14.8k \u8594 ? 24k cells, comparable to CellTypist (4 s \u8594 ? 75 s \u8594 ? 246 s) and somewhat above SVM (9 s \u8594 ? 55 s \u8594 ? 80 s); kNN is lazy (fit is trivial but it pays at predict and is the least accurate). The decisive result is the other axis: {\b actinn-jax\u8217's predict time stays flat and sub-second across the entire range \u8212- ~0.3\u8211-1.4 s whether the reference has 1k or 24k cells, or 5 or 86 types.} Inference cost does not grow with reference size or cardinality, and with the train-once/map-many cache the fit cost is paid once and the flat predict cost is all that recurs \u8212- the regime that matters when a reference is reused across many queries. (The scaling script\u8217's memory column is process-cumulative and not a clean per-size measurement; per-method peak memory is reported from the isolated-subprocess matrix in \u167 ?3.2.)\par}
