{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 Equal accuracy (0.752 vs.\u160 ?0.754, within repeat noise), {\b ~145\u215 ? faster inference} and {\b 3.6\u215 ? less memory} \u8212- the reimplementation is a strict engineering win at no accuracy cost.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel1 \b \fs32 The large\u8594 ?refined annotation workflow\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 The most useful thing actinn-jax ships is not a single classifier but a {\b two-tier workflow} that matches how annotation is actually done: get a broad call fast, then sharpen it where it matters. Because every stage is a cached {\f1 ReferenceModel} with sub-second, memory-bounded inference (\u167 ?3.2, \u167 ?3.4), the whole workflow runs on a laptop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Tier 1 \u8212- broad, census-scale.} A shipped ~800-type human reference built from the CELLxGENE census gives any query a first-pass annotation across the whole body, with a {\b calibrated abstain threshold} so out-of-reference cells are flagged rather than force-labeled (see the smooth abstain curve of \u167 ?3.7). This is the \u8220"what am I looking at\u8221" pass \u8212- broad coverage, deliberately not fine-grained.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Tier 2 \u8212- refined, tissue-focused.} For the tissue the broad pass identifies, a small focused reference re-annotates at full resolution. The gain is large and it is the crux of the workflow: on held-out {\b cross-study} liver cells, the broad census model scores exact-CL {\b 0.23} / ontology {\b 0.58}, while a focused {\b 48-type HLiCA liver} reference on the {\i same cells} reaches {\b 0.72 / 0.86}. Refinement is where fine-grained accuracy comes from; the broad model\u8217's job is to route to it, not to be right about subtypes itself.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Supporting mechanisms.} (i) A {\b foundation-model-guided coarse\u8594 ?fine hierarchy}: a foundation model discovers a coarse\u8594 ?fine structure {\i offline}, the fast CPU model uses it at inference \u8212- beats a flat classifier on 3/4 datasets, matches an expert hierarchy, beats a random-grouping control, and never depends on the foundation model at prediction time. (ii) {\b Within-cell-type resolution}: the same machinery resolves hepatocyte zonation (portal\u8594 ?central) at ~0.99 within-one-zone, generalizing across donors and datasets \u8212- a third tier below the cell-type label. Together these make actinn-jax a {\i pipeline} (broad \u8594 ? tissue \u8594 ? subtype/state), not just a classifier, at classical-method speed throughout.\par}
