{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 actinn-jax {\b ties its {\f1 mlp} sibling on accuracy at ~2\u215 ? the speed} (165 vs 327 s) and {\b beats xgboost\u8217's accuracy at ~5.5\u215 ? less runtime and ~4\u215 ? less memory} (165 s / 21 GB vs 905 s / 81 GB). The faster methods (knn, logistic) are less accurate; the more accurate heavyweight (xgboost) is far slower and heavier \u8212- the same Pareto-non-domination as the in-house benchmark, now on controlled hardware. (An earlier cross-hardware draft overstated the mlp speed gap as \u8220"8\u215 ?\u8221"; the honest same-box figure is ~2\u215 ?.)\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Two cheap ablations, and their honest limits.} (i) {\i Input standardization} \u8212- z-scoring genes to the reference\u8217's frozen mean/std, a CPU-only domain-alignment inspired by scArches reference surgery \u8212- lifts mean accuracy +0.2 and macro-F1 +1.2 pt (largest on batch-shifted datasets: gtex macro-F1 +7.9). It is shipped {\b opt-in} ({\f1 standardize=True}) because it shifts the softmax calibration that the two-stage abstain thresholds are tuned against. (ii) {\i Gene budget} \u8212- OP feeds every method 1000 HVGs, which most starves a gene-space MLP. Widening to ~5000 HVGs lifts accuracy on 4 of 6 datasets (immune/gtex +3 pt) and there {\b matches scANVI+scArches\u8217's full-config leaderboard accuracy} (immune 0.891 \u8776 ? 0.892) on CPU in minutes \u8212- the gap to the top method was largely a gene-budget artifact, not the VAE. But more genes is {\b not} a universal win: it {\i regresses} tabula_sapiens by ~10 pt (its 284-cell test batch across 160 fine types overfits reference-specific genes) and saturates hypomap. Crucially, this is {\b selectable without test labels}: held-out {\i reference} cross-validation rises for the datasets that benefit and is the one signal that {\i drops} for tabula_sapiens, and a trivial query-cells-per-class check independently flags it \u8212- so the budget can be set deterministically per dataset. (iii) {\i Negative control} \u8212- a CPU-only, UCE-style protein-embedding featurization (expression-weighted mean of ESM2 gene embeddings) does {\b not} help and hurts the hardest case: the pooling discards the per-gene detail that separates fine types, so the value of a foundation model like UCE stays locked in its GPU transformer, not a portable averaging trick.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1805\pich724\picwgoal18565\pichgoal7446 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 actinn-jax accuracy and macro-F1 versus input gene budget across all six Open Problems datasets. More genes help most datasets but regress the fine-grained, domain-shifted tabula_sapiens.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 {\pict\pngblip\picw1816\pich781\picwgoal18678\pichgoal8033 }\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li0 \fi0 Label-free signals for setting the gene budget without test labels. Held-out reference cross-validation and query-cells-per-class both single out tabula_sapiens (where more genes hurt).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Discussion\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b actinn-jax is not the accuracy champion, and we do not claim it is.} Deep probabilistic methods and boosted trees edge it by 1\u8211-2 points where they run, both in our panel (\u167 ?3.1) and on Open Problems (\u167 ?3.9); a benchmark that hid this would be wrong. What the evidence supports is narrower and, we argue, more useful: across two independent benchmarks and a controlled same-hardware rerun, {\b no method dominates actinn-jax on both accuracy and cost}, and its differentiators are the ones that decide what a working scientist runs on a laptop.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Speed and footprint.} Inference is {\b sub-second and flat} \u8212- independent of reference size and cardinality (\u167 ?3.4) \u8212- and peak memory sits in the lightest cluster (~2 GB in-house; ~21 GB through OP\u8217's heavier all-layer harness, still less than half of xgboost\u8217's 81 GB on the same box). Against methods of equal accuracy it is 2\u8211-200\u215 ? faster; against methods of equal speed it is more accurate. With the train-once/map-many cache, the flat predict cost is all that recurs when a reference is reused.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i The workflow is the product.} The largest practical gains come not from the flat classifier but from the {\b large\u8594 ?refined} pipeline (\u167 ?3.6): a census-scale broad model with a calibrated abstain routes a query to a small focused reference that re-annotates at full resolution (cross-study liver 0.23/0.58 \u8594 ? 0.72/0.86), with zonation as a further tier. Every stage keeps classical-method speed, so the whole thing runs without a GPU.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Where it loses, and why.} actinn-jax trails on small references and very-few-cells-per-type sets (\u167 ?3.1, \u167 ?5), and \u8212- a finding from the OP analysis \u8212- it is sensitive to input budget under domain shift: more genes help most datasets but overfit a tiny, fine-grained, shifted query (tabula_sapiens). Encouragingly, the two cheapest levers are principled and label-free: {\b standardization} (a scArches-style domain alignment) and a {\b reference-CV-guided gene budget} both improve the model without peeking at test labels, and at a fair gene budget the gene-space MLP {\i matches the leaderboard\u8217's top method} on the datasets both complete \u8212- i.e.\u160 ?much of the apparent gap to heavier methods is representation budget, not model class.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Foundation models.} Their {\b zero-shot labels} are the weakest option in both benchmarks (scPRINT, scGPT, UCE \u8776 ? random); their {\b embeddings/structure} are where the value lies, and our two-stage hierarchy uses exactly that. A cheap CPU shortcut to a foundation-model representation (protein-embedding pooling) did {\i not} transfer (\u167 ?3.9), underscoring that the value is in the trained transformer, not a portable trick.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\i Concurrent evidence that expressivity saturates.} Two independent lines support this reading. First, a head-to-head against {\b ProtoCloud} \u8212- a prototype-based, self-explaining VAE with built-in uncertainty and gene-level attribution, i.e.\u160 ?a strictly richer model than ours \u8212- is close and splits both ways: mean accuracy 0.867 (actinn-jax) vs 0.837 across four of our datasets, ProtoCloud ahead on the finest-grained set, actinn-jax training ~8.5\u215 ? faster on CPU ({\f1 PROTOCLOUD.md}). Second, and more broadly, Souza & Mehta report that {\b parameter-free linear representations} match or exceed single-cell foundation models across cross-species transfer, human cell-type classification and disease-state prediction \u8212- on Tabula Sapiens 2.0 their normalize\u8594 ?ANOVA\u8594 ?PCA\u8594 ?logistic-regression pipeline reaches mean macro-F1 0.899 against 0.907\u8211-0.910 for TranscriptFormer variants \u8212- and they offer a geometric explanation: the biologically realized cell manifold is well approximated by a {\b linear subspace}, so once noise is suppressed performance {\i saturates} and extra expressivity buys little. That is a principled account of why a four-layer MLP over normalized gene space stays competitive, and it matches our own finding that the residual gap to heavier methods was mostly {\b representation budget, not model class} (\u167 ?3.9). Their further result that simple methods do {\i best} out-of-distribution \u8212- novel cell types and unseen organisms \u8212- also rhymes with the rejection behavior we depend on (\u167 ?3.7).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 {\b Practical guidance.} For a {\b one-off annotation} on laptop-sized data, reach for the {\b tuned linear pipeline} (normalize \u8594 ? ANOVA \u8594 ? PCA \u8594 ? logistic regression): it is the most accurate-per-second method here and beats actinn-jax outright on accuracy and fit time. If you have a {\b real atlas and time to train}, {\b ProtoCloud} is the most accurate method we benchmarked (0.976 at 49k cells), and its GPU target makes its 19\u215 ?-CPU fit cost far less punishing in practice. {\b scTOP} is the right tool only for small, low-cardinality problems, where it is dramatically the cheapest (1\u8211-2 s) and competitive. {\b actinn-jax} is the choice when {\b memory is the binding constraint} (2.1\u215 ? lighter than the linear pipeline at scale, with memory-bounded chunked inference), when the reference is {\b reused} across many queries (the cached model amortizes; the linear pipeline refits scaler/PCA/classifier each time), or when you want the {\b workflow} \u8212- broad\u8594 ?refined hand-off, tissue-aware refinement, calibrated abstain, novel-cell-type screening. Of these, the {\b ready-to-run broad reference} and {\b sub-second frozen-model refinement} have no counterpart in the baselines; ProtoCloud does provide uncertainty, gene attribution, and a refinement path (by fine-tuning a pretrained model \u8212- stronger than masking, but a training run), so we do not claim workflow exclusivity.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa180 \li0 \fi0 \outlinelevel0 \b \fs36 Limitations\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Single hardware family for the in-house panel (Apple Silicon); no discrete-GPU numbers there (deep/foundation tiers would be faster on CUDA \u8212- out of scope for the \u8220"runs-on-a-laptop\u8221" question). The \u167 ?3.9 controlled run covers the {\b CPU tier} on one cloud box; the GPU/R methods there are reported from OP\u8217's own cloud-CI trace (indicative). A same-hardware GPU-tier run to fold those into a single controlled table is the natural extension.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Subsampled references (to keep the matrix tractable); the scaling section characterizes the size dependence directly.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab Human only; six datasets per benchmark; GPU foundation models beyond scPRINT/UCE (scGPT, Geneformer, popV) not run locally.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab actinn-jax needs more cells/type than linear methods to reach parity (\u167 ?3.1); on very small references it trails.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b The gene budget is dataset-dependent, not a free parameter.} More genes help most references but {\i overfit} a tiny, fine-grained, domain-shifted query (tabula_sapiens \u8722 ?10 pt); our reference-CV + cells-per-class selection rule is validated on 6 datasets with a single clean failure case, so it is directional evidence, not a tuned threshold.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Standardization is shipped opt-in}, not default, because it shifts probability calibration that the two-stage abstain thresholds are tuned against; combining the two cleanly would require re-tuning those thresholds.\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b A tuned linear baseline is more accurate than actinn-jax (measured, all four datasets).} Our classical tier (SVM, kNN, CellTypist) is untuned. Adding Souza & Mehta\u8217's pipeline (normalize \u8594 ? ANOVA \u8594 ? standardize \u8594 ? PCA(220) \u8594 ? logistic regression) and {\b scTOP} ({\f1 SIMPLE_BASELINES.md}) shows the linear pipeline {\b beats actinn-jax on mean accuracy (0.880 vs 0.867) and macro-F1 (0.860 vs 0.839) while fitting 3.7\u215 ? faster} \u8212- a tie on pbmc3k, slight edges on lung and liver, and {\b +4.2 pt on the 86-type blood+gut set} (0.902 vs 0.860). It also beats ProtoCloud on 3 of 4. The \u167 ?3.1 margins therefore reflect {\i untuned} baselines, and {\b actinn-jax\u8217's case rests on cost profile and workflow, not accuracy}: bounded sparse memory (1.7 GB vs 3.6 GB mean, 2.6 vs 5.5 GB on blood+gut, and the gap grows because ANOVA/PCA densify cells \u215 ? genes), the cached reusable reference, and broad\u8594 ?refined / abstain / novel-detection. For one-shot labels on laptop-sized data, the linear pipeline is the better default. scTOP is cheaper still (1.2 s, 1.4 GB) with the best pbmc3k macro-F1 (0.837), but degrades with cardinality (0.910 \u8594 ? 0.828 \u8594 ? 0.795; 0.649 on liver).\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b At atlas scale the deep model wins, and our memory argument is smaller than claimed} ({\f1 SCALING_MEMORY.md}). Scaling the lung reference 3k \u8594 ? 49k cells:\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li720 \fi-360 (i)\tx360\tab {\b all four methods complete} \u8212- we had projected the linear pipeline would need ~32 GB and fail; it used {\b 13.2 GB}, so that projection is retracted; (ii) the linear/actinn-jax memory ratio is {\b bounded at ~2\u8211-3\u215 ? and does not diverge} (2.15\u215 ? at full scale), so there is no scaling cliff; (iii) {\b ProtoCloud goes from worst (0.722 at 3k) to best (0.976 at 49k)}, well clear of actinn-jax (0.936) and the linear pipeline (0.939) \u8212- our earlier \u8220"accuracy is close\u8221" reading was an artifact of subsampling, and a data-hungry VAE pulls ahead once given a real atlas (at 19\u215 ? CPU fit cost); (iv) scTOP does not benefit from scale at all (0.819 \u8594 ? 0.846) and {\i loses} its memory edge (1.22\u215 ? actinn-jax at 49k). {\b actinn-jax is not the accuracy leader at any scale tested here.}\sa180\par}
{\pard \ql \f0 \sa0 \li360 \fi-360 \bullet \tx360\tab {\b Annotation only.} Cross-species transfer and disease-state prediction \u8212- the tasks where Souza & Mehta find the largest foundation-model deficits, and where a fast method would be most attractive \u8212- are outside this benchmark\u8217's scope (human, within/cross-dataset annotation).\sa180\par}
